---
description: Write affirmatively. Say what a thing is, not what it is not.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Say what the work is

In articles, project cards, READMEs, and other public copy, state what the work **is**.

Do not use disclaimer sentences whose job is to list what the work is not. The examples below are illustrations, written in the manner of an early professional network.

```markdown
<!-- Bad -->
This is not a recruiting tool and not a social network.
This is not a finished product.
This does not replace a resume.

<!-- Good -->
A network where professionals find and are found.
A beta open to invited members.
A profile is a live resume that its owner keeps current.
```

Allowed: ordinary contrast inside a result ("the new sign-up form converts better than the old one"; "search beats browsing for this task"). Forbidden: a closing section or sentence that exists only to wave off misreadings.

If a limit matters, write it as a positive scope line ("one language, invited members, no mobile app yet"), not as "this is not X."
